An and Ea from Sumerian mythology seem to have non-trivial roles in the proceedings. What I haven't figured out is whether these were actually considered at the time to be the same figures as in the Mesopotamian tradition, or whether this is just Sumerograms causing problems. Does anyone here have a more detailed knowledge of the subject that they'd be willing to share?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sacerdos and I actually spent all day today working on the Hittites, so I can probably give a decent explanation of what is going on here. He will be able to explain far better than I will however, so hopefully he will be able to pop in and correct what I get wrong here. In short, it's a Sumerogram problem mixed in with a lot of our Hittite sources being horrifically damaged leaving us with limited names for figures.<br /> <br /> While the Hittites were super keen on identifying their own Deities with ones of conquered peoples, they very pointedly did <i>not</i> do this with others who they had not conquered. For instance, they absolutely recognized that the Netjer existed, and imagined that the Netjer and the \u0160iune\u0161 both were divine guarantors of their international alliance, that they were a different group of divinities. If they conquered someone, the state policy was synchronism. I would say that if the Hittites had managed to properly conquer Mesopotamia (they gave it a good crack, never quite managed it though. <i>Almost</i> nicked Marduk's cult statue but had to abandon it) they would have probably started merging the entities. But, as they never did, the Hittites would have regarded them as different beings as the Hittites needed all of their neighbors to have unique sets of deities for any of their peace treaties or trade deals to 'work' from their perspective. They would have had their own names for An (Hittite) and Ea (Hittite), but we have just lost the names, just as you suspect.<br /> <br /> For a more detailed breakdown, An (Hitt) and An (Meso) seem pretty similar as &quot;Distant lost kings who have something to do with the sky,&quot; but I think they are pretty different in their charactarization. We only see An (Hitt) for a few lines, but he totally wins the &quot;Absurdly Absentee Father,&quot; award of mythology. If you look at An (Meso), An (Hitt) and Ouranos, you can see this really cool transition however as An (Hitt) has features from both (though is way older than Ouranos).<br /> <br /> The really big differences appear with Ea (Hit) and Ea (Meso) however. Ea (Hit) is the source of most of the Hittite Titanomauchy as he wanders around as an Absurdly Powerful Kingmaker who just can't stop meddling in the affairs of the younger deities. Ea (Meso) on the other hand works with his relatives to deal with Abzu. Both of them are on different sides, and both of them are on different sides of the Divine Generations, with Ea (Hitt) being one of their Primeval Gods, and Ea (Meso) being one of their normal, younger generation Gods. They <i>do</i> both have something to do with Knowledge, but Ea (Hitt) gets all his from an informant simply known as &quot;Ea's Beast,&quot; and Ea (Meso) just is a smart cookie. Ea (Hitt) is an agent of absolute order, trying to find the correct king to rule, where Ea (Meso) is a chaotic little guy who one day will be wandering around all kingly, and the next day will be causing problems like accidentally getting pregnant, pulled a Confusion Of Language because he could, defying the will of the rightful ruler Enlil by saving a human from the Deluge.<br /> <br /> So, you are totally right that it is a Sumerogram problem. In Scion, I would normally have Ea (Hitt) be irate that people forgot his original name and <i>refuse</i> on principle to tell anyone what it is since he's like that, and An (Hitt), well, I don't think he would show up for anything to have a relevant opinion. The Anunna would probably accuse the \u0160iune\u0161 of plagiarism or something.<br /> <br /> I totally hope 